        <div id="diameter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Diameter: Why Bigger Is Better for a Cat’s Back</h2>
<p>
            The inside of a wheel is a curve, and a cat running on it bends to that curve. On a small
            wheel, the curve is tight, and a large cat has to run with its back arched and its stride
            shortened. On a large wheel, the running surface under the cat is close to flat, and it can
            stretch out much as it would on the floor. That is why the makers of the larger wheels on
            this page talk so much about the spine.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is no published standard, but the listings give a consistent picture. One Fast Cat,
            which sells its wheel as the original cat exercise wheel, makes its wheel 48 inches across and says that
            size accommodates cats up to 25 lb with plenty of room to extend their bodies. Nordic
            Beast also uses 48 inches and rates its wider version for cats up to 45 lb. Smaller wheels,
            around 30 to 40 inches, are sold for smaller cats or kittens; one 29.5 inch wheel we looked
            at, but did not include, rated itself for cats up to only 8 lb.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Our suggestion:</strong> for an average adult cat, aim for around 48 inches. For a
            large breed such as a Maine Coon, Norwegian Forest Cat, Ragdoll or Savannah, choose 48
            inches with a wide running surface. A wheel of about 40 inches suits smaller cats and
            saves space. Below that, think of it as a kitten or small-cat wheel.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Running width</h3>
          <p>
            The width of the track matters for big cats and for households where two cats might use
            the wheel at once. Most wheels have a track of 12 to 14 inches; Nordic Beast's is 15.7
            inches, which it says fits two cats side by side. A wider track also gives a nervous cat
            more room to place its feet.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Weight ratings</h3>
          <p>
            Treat weight ratings with care. Some are realistic, such as One Fast Cat's 25 lb or Nordic
            Beast's 45 lb. Others, such as 110 lb or 150 lb on wheels on this page, describe the
            strength of the frame rather than the size of cat the wheel suits; no house cat weighs
            that much. As with dog treadmills, go by size, not by the weight rating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-and-lock" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Noise, Locks and Brakes</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Noise.</strong> A cat wheel turns on rollers in the base, and the rollers make the
            noise. Several listings on this page describe rubber-like TPE rollers or bearings meant to
            run quietly; Nordic Beast describes the sound as a gentle rolling, like a skateboard. A
            wheel will never be silent, and cats often run hardest at dawn and late at night. If the
            wheel will be near a bedroom, choose one that describes quiet rollers, put it on a rug or
            a dense mat rather than a bare wooden floor, and keep it level so it does not rock. In an
            apartment, the same rules about structure-borne noise apply as for any exercise
            equipment; our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apartments/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for apartments</a> explains how vibration travels.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Locks.</strong> Three of the wheels on this page have a lock or latch that stops
            the wheel turning. A lock is useful in three ways. It lets a nervous cat explore a wheel
            that does not move. It lets you control when the wheel is used, so it is not in use at
            three in the morning or when nobody is home. And it holds the wheel still while a cat
            climbs in and out, which reduces the chance of a stumble. One Fast Cat's and Nordic
            Beast's listings do not mention a lock.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Brakes.</strong> Cat wheels have no brake in the sense a dog slatmill does; the
            cat slows the wheel by slowing down. A wheel that keeps spinning after a cat jumps off can
            catch a second cat or a small child, which is another reason to lock it when you are not
            there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="training" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Train a Cat to Use a Wheel</h2>
<p>
            Some cats step onto a wheel and run the first day. Most need a few days to a couple of
            weeks, and some never take to it. One Fast Cat says most cats can learn with a bit of
            daily training over a couple of weeks, and that older or low-energy cats may need more.
            The method that most owners and makers describe is the same: short sessions, a toy to
            chase, and a reward for every step.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Let the cat investigate.</strong> Put the wheel where the cat already spends
            time, lock it if it has a lock, and leave it for a day or two. Put treats or catnip on the
            track. Let the cat climb in, sit and sleep in it.</li>
            <li><strong>Teach one step.</strong> Unlock the wheel and, with the cat inside, use a
            treat or a wand toy just ahead of its nose to coax one step. The wheel will move a little.
            Reward immediately. If the movement startles the cat, go back to the locked wheel for
            another day.</li>
            <li><strong>Build to walking.</strong> Over a few sessions, lead the cat further with the
            wand, rewarding each few steps. Many owners use a clicker or a word to mark the moment the
            cat moves the wheel, followed by a treat.</li>
            <li><strong>Add speed with play.</strong> Once the cat walks the wheel, move the wand
            faster along the front of the track so it trots and then runs after it. Keep sessions to a
            few minutes and stop while the cat is still keen.</li>
            <li><strong>End with a catch.</strong> If you use a light pointer, finish the session by
            letting the cat catch a real toy or a treat, so the game ends with a win rather than
            frustration.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Never push, hold or shut a cat in the wheel. A cat that is forced will learn to avoid it.
            If your cat shows no interest after a few weeks of short, positive sessions, accept that
            this cat is not a wheel cat; play sessions with a wand toy give much of the same exercise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Safety: Paws, Kittens and Older Cats</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>Check the gap.</strong> The gap between the wheel and the base is where paws
            and tails can be pinched. Several listings here describe a narrowed gap or pinch-prevention
            design. Check it after assembly, and again after a few weeks, because screws loosen.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep it level and stable.</strong> A wheel that rocks or walks across the
            floor can tip. Put it on a flat surface, check that the base sits evenly, and keep it clear
            of furniture the cat could jump onto it from.</li>
            <li><strong>Supervise at first.</strong> Watch the first few weeks of sessions. Lock the
            wheel, if it locks, when nobody is home.</li>
            <li><strong>Kittens.</strong> Some listings say their wheels suit kittens from a young
            age. Kittens are clumsy and growing; keep sessions short, supervised and playful.</li>
            <li><strong>Older, heavy or arthritic cats.</strong> Ask your vet first. A wheel can help
            an overweight cat, but a sudden increase in exercise is not right for every cat, and a
            cat with joint pain may need a different plan.</li>
            <li><strong>Other pets and children.</strong> A spinning wheel can catch a small dog's
            paw or a child's fingers. Teach children not to spin it, and lock it when unattended.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Never put a cat on a motorised treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            A human treadmill or a motorised dog treadmill is not a safe way to exercise a cat. The
            belt moves whether or not the cat wants it to, cats can panic and be thrown off the back,
            and paws, fur and tails can be caught in the gaps at the ends of the belt. Keep cats away
            from household treadmills altogether, and unplug and remove the safety key after use. Our <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety guide</a> covers keeping pets away from a running machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Space, Assembly and Placement</h2>
<p>
            A 48 inch wheel needs a footprint of about four feet long and 12 to 16 inches deep, and it
            stands over four feet tall. Leave clear space on both sides for the cat to approach and jump off,
            and keep it away from shelves and windowsills the cat could launch from onto the moving
            wheel.
          </p>
          <p>
            Assembly ranges from snapping lightweight parts together, as One Fast Cat describes, to
            screwing together a heavy wooden frame, which several of the others need. The wooden and
            plywood wheels on this page weigh from about 30 to 50 lb assembled, so build them where
            they will stand.
          </p>
          <p>
            On a wooden or laminate floor, a mat under the base reduces noise and protects the floor
            from the base shifting. A dense equipment mat works better than a soft rug; our guide to <a href="/best-treadmill-mats-for-hardwood-floors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mats for hardwood floors</a> covers which materials are safe on which floors.
          </p>
          <p>
            Clean the track regularly. Felt and carpet tracks collect fur; foam shows claw marks. Most
            wheels here have a removable carpet or pad that can be vacuumed or wiped, and some sellers
            sell replacement pads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Not Buy a Cat Wheel</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If your cat is elderly, arthritic or recovering from injury</strong>, talk to your
            vet before buying anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have very little space</strong>, a large wheel will dominate a small room.
            A climbing tree and daily wand-toy play give much of the benefit in less space.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your cat is already very active</strong> and has plenty of space to run and
            climb, a wheel may not add much.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you were hoping to exercise a dog as well</strong>, a cat wheel is not the
            answer; see our roundup of <a href="/best-dog-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dog treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes With Cat Treadmills</h2>
          <p>Each of these either puts the cat off the wheel or puts it at risk.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a wheel too small for the cat</h3>
              <p>A small wheel forces a large cat to run with an arched back and a short stride, and many will simply refuse it. Go by diameter, not by the weight rating: about 48 inches for an average adult cat and a wide 48 inch wheel for a big breed.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forcing the cat in</h3>
              <p>Holding a cat in the wheel or shutting it in teaches it to avoid the wheel. Let it explore a locked or still wheel first, reward every step, and keep sessions short and playful.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaving it unlocked when nobody is home</h3>
              <p>A spinning wheel can catch a second cat, a small dog's paw or a child's fingers. If your wheel has a lock, use it when you are out, at least until you know how your pets behave around it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Using a motorised treadmill instead</h3>
              <p>A human or dog treadmill is not a cat treadmill. The belt keeps moving when the cat wants to stop, and the gaps at the ends can catch paws and tails. Use a self-powered wheel only.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Buy by diameter, then by lock.</strong> One Fast Cat's 48 inch
            wheel is the original for cats up to 25 lb; Nordic Beast's wide XL wheel suits large breeds
            and two-cat homes; Homegroove and POLAHAKU add a lock to a large wheel, POLAHAKU for about
            $80.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, train with short, positive sessions, lock it when nobody is home, and
            never put a cat on a motorised belt. If you have a dog to exercise too, see our roundup of <a href="/best-dog-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">dog treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

