        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Do cat treadmills actually work?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Many cats learn to use an exercise wheel, and once they do, it gives indoor cats a way to burn energy on their own schedule. Not every cat takes to one. Most need short, positive training sessions over a few days to a couple of weeks, and some never use it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What size cat wheel do I need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For an average adult cat, about 48 inches in diameter, so the cat can run with its back close to straight. For a large breed, 48 inches with a wide track. Wheels around 40 inches suit smaller cats; smaller still suit kittens or very small cats only.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a cat use a regular treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. A motorised treadmill moves whether or not the cat wants to, cats can be thrown off the back, and paws and tails can be caught at the ends of the belt. Use a self-powered cat wheel instead, and keep cats away from household treadmills.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are cat exercise wheels noisy?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They make a rolling sound from the rollers in the base, which varies by design. Wheels with rubber-like TPE rollers or bearings are described as quieter. Put the wheel on a rug or dense mat, keep it level, and use the lock at night if it has one.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long does it take to train a cat to use a wheel?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              One Fast Cat says most cats learn with a bit of daily training over a couple of weeks, and older or low-energy cats may need longer. Start with the wheel locked or still, reward each step with treats, then use a wand toy to lead the cat into walking and running.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a cat wheel safe for kittens and older cats?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Kittens can use one with short, supervised, playful sessions. For older, overweight or arthritic cats, ask your vet first. For any cat, check the gap between wheel and base for pinch points and lock the wheel when nobody is home.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

